---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PanelHead from '../components/PanelHead.astro';
import spec from '../../method/method.md?raw';

// Hand-written copy for a reader, ASSERTED against the spec (chooser pick 04-03). The
// alternative was rendering method.md through a markdown renderer: zero drift, but it
// publishes an agent-facing document — install instructions and "load the side the task
// needs" — to a human, and adds a dependency to a site that has none. So the page is
// edited, and the sentences it shares with the spec are checked at build time. A typo in
// the spec breaks the build; that is the intended behaviour.
//
// Boundary of what this checks: the one sentence this page still quotes verbatim, not the
// tone of the paragraphs around it. Whitespace is collapsed because the markdown wraps
// mid-sentence.
//
// The thesis sentence used to be checked here too. It came out when the opening paragraphs
// were reset in plain first person: the page now states the same idea as procedure ("I ask
// the same question on both sides of the work") instead of quoting the aphorism, so
// asserting it here would guard a string the page no longer prints. The index still
// displays it verbatim and still asserts it — see the THESIS check in index.astro.
const SHARED = [
  'Not a biography, not a résumé, not a decision log, and not a stand-in for a person.',
];
const flat = spec.replace(/\s+/g, ' ');
for (const s of SHARED) {
  if (!flat.includes(s)) {
    throw new Error('method/method.md no longer contains a sentence /method/ quotes:\n  ' + s);
  }
}
---

<Layout
  title="Method — Andrew Shiau"
  description="One principle, two instruments: how a design decision gets made, how a change gets proven, and how the result leaves my hands."
>
  <!-- THE SPLIT (chooser round 2: concept 02, scope 03). This page was the tenth and it
       was missed: the index's feed opens with "Method → The operating spec", so the first
       click off the landing page used to leave the new shape for the old full-width stack.
       Same `.pin` / `.splitrule` / `.feed` children at the same 4:8 columns as everything
       else, so the site's one vertical division doesn't move.
       TYPE B: the facts block here is a COLOPHON — Form, What it isn't, Source, the same
       shape as `aping`'s — so it stays at the foot of the reading column and the panel is
       marker + title + Type + contact. Not Type A: this page has no role and no employer
       to print, because it isn't a project. -->
  <div class="grid">
    <PanelHead href="/method/" title="Method" />

    <div class="feed">
      <!-- c1–12 of the FEED's twelve sub-tracks, matching every other page in the split:
           the dek's own 32em cap is narrower than 8 of the feed's 700px. -->
      <div class="grid">
        <p class="dek c1-12">
          How I make a design decision, how I prove a change works, and how either one
          leaves my hands — written down as procedure. Both halves ask the same question: is
          this the simplest thing that works? One answers it by looking, the other by
          measuring.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- The unified statement, before the split. The two columns below only make sense as
           two halves of one thing, and a reader who meets them cold reads them as two
           unrelated lists — so the shared claim runs first.
           It gets a NAMED head, like every other block of prose on the site. It used to be
           two headless paragraphs, which is the same defect that was fixed on `aping` in
           this pass: it made the best writing on the page look like a stray thought.
           Voice: plain first person (round-3 sheet, Q03-B). This paragraph used to run
           "That is not two philosophies. It is one principle with two instruments" and close
           on "a form nobody looked at isn't designed" — an antithesis snap and an aphorism,
           both banned by STYLE.md, and both stating a principle where the procedure below
           states a fact. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>Why it’s one method</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              I ask the same question on both sides of the work. On a design I answer it by
              rendering the options in the real system and looking at them. On code I answer it
              by measuring against a control. When the two disagree I go with the measurement,
              and I write down which one it was.
            </p>
            <p>
              This is the part I bring to a team. The procedures below are the ones I run at
              work, on a system where a wrong component ships to a lot of pages at once — and
              they’re written as a spec an agent can execute, so a second person, or a machine,
              can run them without me in the room.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- The duality as the page's shape (chooser pick 02): two procedures side by side,
           each under its own hue. The composition states the argument before a word is read.
           Inside the feed the two columns stack — see `.feed .duo` in global.css for the
           measurement and the cost. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="c1-12"><h2>Two instruments</h2></div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="duo">
          <div class="dcol d">
            <span class="dk">Design — the instrument is the eye</span>
            <h3>How a design decision gets made</h3>
            <ol>
              <li><b>Render every option, in the real system</b> — the actual typeface,
                tokens, grid, and breakpoints it ships in.</li>
              <li><b>Option 1 is always what’s live today.</b> Without the baseline,
                “better” has no referent.</li>
              <li>A few distinct options beat a dozen near-duplicates. If two differ by
                2px, they are one option.</li>
              <li><b>Pick by looking.</b> Then state the one-line reason — after the
                choice, not before.</li>
              <li>Check both colourways and phone width before calling it done.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <div class="dcol b">
            <span class="dk">Build — the instrument is measurement</span>
            <h3>How a change gets proven</h3>
            <ol>
              <li><b>Prove it on the real deployment.</b> A unit test and a mockup are
                both hypotheses.</li>
              <li><b>Verify the change is in the live artifact</b> before trusting a
                result. A failure against a stale bundle is a false negative.</li>
              <li><b>Pair every change with a control.</b> A/B proves the change took;
                A/A proves nothing else moved.</li>
              <li>A visual signal when it renders, a trace when it doesn’t — the cheapest
                observation that can actually fail.</li>
              <li><b>Report the boundary</b> of what was tested, and what wasn’t.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- The one filled form on the page, for the sentence that joins the two columns.
           NO INLINE MARGIN. It carried `margin-top:var(--group)`, which an inline style put
           ahead of `.grid:has(> .block)`'s `--sect` — and since the block's own 112 could not
           collapse with it (grid items don't), the two stacked to 168px above and 224px below.
           See `.block` in global.css for the measurement. The interval is the shared rule's now. -->
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="block c1-6">
          <span class="st">Both sides need a baseline</span>
          <span class="sd">In design the baseline is what’s live today, rendered beside the
            alternatives. In engineering it’s the control arm of an A/B. Without one I can’t
            tell whether a candidate is better or just different.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- THE THIRD SIDE (chooser: shape 02, framing 02). Deliberately NOT a third column
           in the `.duo` above: design and build are the two ways of DECIDING, and this is
           what happens to whatever they produce, so it reads after the pair rather than
           beside it. A 4/4/4 duo would also have cut each column to ~1/3 measure, and the
           CSS comment on `.duo` already notes the 2-up tightens badly in the 700px feed.
           Type + rule, no filled `.block` — the one filled form on this page is the
           baseline statement above (STYLE.md: once per page). Same `<ol>` as the two
           columns, so the procedure reads as a peer of the other two procedures even
           though the framing says it comes after them. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="c1-12"><h2>Then the handoff</h2></div>
      </div>
      <!-- Inside a `.duo` with ONE column, not a bare `.grid` child: `.dk`, `h3` and `ol`
           are all scoped to `.duo` in global.css, so a copy of this markup outside it
           renders an unstyled kicker and a default-leading heading. One column at span 8
           reuses the three rules verbatim and adds no selector. -->
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="duo">
          <div class="dcol" style="grid-column:span 8">
          <span class="dk">Messaging — the instrument is the sentence</span>
          <h3>How the work leaves my hands</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><b>Answer first.</b> A reader builds a hierarchy out of whatever order I
              hand them, so I hand them the true one. The order I found something in is
              almost never the order to explain it.</li>
            <li><b>If I can’t say it plainly, I haven’t finished thinking it.</b> The
              write-up is the last place the work gets checked, not a wrapper round it.</li>
            <li><b>Say the strong thing, then say how sure I am.</b> Confidence and accuracy
              are separate dials. Naming what I don’t know usually buys more trust than the
              finding it qualifies.</li>
            <li>The artifact carries the detail; the message carries the ask. If it’s
              linked, I don’t summarise it.</li>
            <li><b>Cut the part I’m proud of.</b> The detail hardest to remove is the one
              that cost the most to find, and that cost isn’t the reader’s.</li>
          </ol>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- The five rules that apply to both sides (chooser pick 03), as .step cells: the
           last module stays empty, so the row reads as the composition rather than as a
           list that ran out. Rule 01 takes the accent rule — it's the one the rest follow
           from, and it's the one this site had to obey to earn the page.
           One `.rules` grid, not two `.grid` rows with hand-written `grid-column` spans:
           inline spans can't be re-cut for the 700px feed without `!important`, and the
           3-up the old markup hard-coded is 155px per cell in there. See `.rules` in
           global.css — 3-up at full measure, 2-up in the feed, one column on a phone. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="c1-12"><h2>Rules for both</h2></div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="rules">
          <div class="step accent">
            <span class="sl">Rule 01</span>
            <span class="st">Simplify by removal, not by hiding</span>
            <span class="sd">The win is the thing that’s gone — a step, a file, a person in the
              loop — not a thing tucked behind a disclosure triangle.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <span class="sl">Rule 02</span>
            <span class="st">Decide by looking or by measuring</span>
            <span class="sd">Never by arguing. If a decision is being settled in prose, the
              wrong instrument is in use. Render it, or run it.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <span class="sl">Rule 03</span>
            <span class="st">Real conditions or it doesn’t count</span>
            <span class="sd">The actual tokens and grid; the actual deployment, not a fixture.
              A mockup and a unit test are both hypotheses.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <span class="sl">Rule 04</span>
            <span class="st">Name the mechanism</span>
            <span class="sd">“Probably a race” is not a diagnosis. Either state the causal
              chain or say plainly that you don’t have it yet.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <span class="sl">Rule 05</span>
            <span class="st">State what was <i>not</i> tested</span>
            <span class="sd">Partial verification reported as complete is the failure mode that
              costs the most.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE B close: the facts block as a colophon — what the thing is made of, read
           after the work, the same position it takes on `aping` and `explain`. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="facts">
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">Form</div>
            <div class="ftx">
              Four markdown files in this site’s repo — <b>method.md</b> and one per side.
              A pointer file loads the root into every agent session I run; the side gets
              loaded when the task needs it.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">What it isn’t</div>
            <div class="ftx">
              Not a biography, not a résumé, not a decision log, and not a stand-in for a
              person. It encodes <b>how to decide</b>, so a fresh agent can run the procedures
              rather than recite the facts.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">Source</div>
            <div class="ftx">
              <a href="https://github.com/halfcadence/andrewshiau.com/tree/main/method"
                 target="_blank" rel="noopener">the four files on GitHub</a> — the same text
              this page is checked against at build time.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
